WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.190 --> 00:00:06.020
THE YOUTUBE SPONSORSHIP CRISIS:
YouTube has a sponsorship problem.

00:00:06.020 --> 00:00:11.250
Once upon a time, YouTubers used to pitch
products and services that they truly used,

00:00:11.250 --> 00:00:13.200
enjoyed, and believed in.

00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:18.869
But, today, YouTubers pitch just about anything
from questionable products like Skillshare

00:00:18.869 --> 00:00:24.770
and Better Help to straight up scams like
FTX, Luna, and NFTs.

00:00:24.770 --> 00:00:26.750
And it’s not surprising why.

00:00:26.750 --> 00:00:30.859
The amounts that these sponsors offer is downright
stupid.

00:00:30.859 --> 00:00:36.500
Remeber the FTX collapse, well I’m a relatively
small channel, and I was offered $30,000 to

00:00:36.500 --> 00:00:39.190
promote some sort of Shiba Inu bailout fund.

00:00:39.190 --> 00:00:45.090
That’s of course an extreme example, but
I’m regularly offered amounts of $5 to $10,000

00:00:45.090 --> 00:00:48.980
from more tame sponsors several times every
single day.

00:00:48.980 --> 00:00:54.000
Given that I post about a 150 videos every
year, if I did sponsors, that could be an

00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:58.829
extra $750,000 to $1,500,000 every single
year.

00:00:58.829 --> 00:01:03.730
So, it’s not surprising why so many YouTubers
put their morals aside and start shilling

00:01:03.730 --> 00:01:04.939
for these sponsors.

00:01:04.939 --> 00:01:10.330
Now, don’t get me wrong here, I don’t
hate sponsors or YouTubers who use sponsors.

00:01:10.330 --> 00:01:16.470
In fact, if done the right way, I think sponsors
can benefit the YouTubers, the sponsors, and

00:01:16.470 --> 00:01:17.630
the audience.

00:01:17.630 --> 00:01:23.210
The problem is that in 99% of cases, the audience
is left out of the picture.

00:01:23.210 --> 00:01:29.329
In fact, the intended purpose of the ad is
often for the audience to literally get scammed.

00:01:29.329 --> 00:01:34.909
And even if the sponsor is a reputable company,
it’s questionable how much value the audience

00:01:34.909 --> 00:01:36.490
is actually receiving.

00:01:36.490 --> 00:01:40.050
Doesn’t everybody just skip through the
sponsored segments anyway?

00:01:40.050 --> 00:01:45.140
I mean, why would you watch them when they’re
virtually indiscernible from regular ad slots

00:01:45.140 --> 00:01:46.820
that play before a video.

00:01:46.820 --> 00:01:51.130
The only difference is that it’s the YouTuber
pitching the product as opposed to some random

00:01:51.130 --> 00:01:52.130
actor.

00:01:52.130 --> 00:01:57.060
But, don’t be fooled, this doesn’t mean
that the YouTuber is any more knowledge or

00:01:57.060 --> 00:02:00.140
familiar with the product than some random
actor.

00:02:00.140 --> 00:02:04.740
When things go to crap like with FTX, YouTubers
will be the first one to shift the blame to

00:02:04.740 --> 00:02:05.740
you.

00:02:05.740 --> 00:02:09.160
They’ll say something like, at the end of
the day, it’s up to the viewer to determine

00:02:09.160 --> 00:02:11.129
whether to use a sponsored product.

00:02:11.129 --> 00:02:16.580
And that’s fair, but if that’s how YouTubers
are gonna approach sponsors, why should you

00:02:16.580 --> 00:02:20.660
as a viewer give sponsors any more importance
than a random ad.

00:02:20.660 --> 00:02:24.790
The answer is that you shouldn’t and that’s
probably for the best.

00:02:24.790 --> 00:02:27.900
But, it wasn’t always like this though.

00:02:27.900 --> 00:02:33.450
At one point it in time, sponsors were actually
enhancing content on YouTube and adding value

00:02:33.450 --> 00:02:35.120
to the audience.

00:02:35.120 --> 00:02:39.420
And there are still a select number of YouTubers
for whom this is still true.

00:02:39.420 --> 00:02:44.940
So, here’s how sponsors devolved from being
a valuable production tool to being a shameless

00:02:44.940 --> 00:02:47.280
money grab.

00:02:47.280 --> 00:02:57.460
GOOD SPONSORSHIPS:
Before we get into bad sponsorships, let’s

00:02:57.460 --> 00:03:01.280
first establish what a good sponsorship implementation
looks like.

00:03:01.280 --> 00:03:06.120
One of the best examples of sponsorships being
used well is none other than Mr. Beast.

00:03:06.120 --> 00:03:10.909
For one, he thoroughly vets each and every
one of his sponsorships, but that in itself

00:03:10.909 --> 00:03:12.680
doesn’t make a good sponsor.

00:03:12.680 --> 00:03:17.209
I mean, just because you get sponsored by
a reputable company doesn’t mean that it’s

00:03:17.209 --> 00:03:19.010
actually valuable to the audience.

00:03:19.010 --> 00:03:23.390
Now, you could go down the rabbit hole of
determining whether your audience demographic

00:03:23.390 --> 00:03:27.780
matches the demographic of the sponsor, and
what type of topics you are audience is interested

00:03:27.780 --> 00:03:28.780
in, and all of that.

00:03:28.780 --> 00:03:32.480
You should also make the sponsored segment
as engaging as possible and maximize audience

00:03:32.480 --> 00:03:34.010
retention and all of that.

00:03:34.010 --> 00:03:39.150
But, there’s a much simpler way to determine
if a sponsorship implementation is good or

00:03:39.150 --> 00:03:40.150
not.

00:03:40.150 --> 00:03:44.720
The key to a good sponsorship is that it should
offer value even to viewers who skip the sponsorship

00:03:44.720 --> 00:03:45.720
completely.

00:03:45.720 --> 00:03:49.459
But, wait a minute, how could that be possible?

00:03:49.459 --> 00:03:53.900
If a viewer doesn’t watch the sponsor, how
could they possible get value out of the products

00:03:53.900 --> 00:03:56.290
or services offered by the sponsor.

00:03:56.290 --> 00:04:00.140
Well, easy, from the video that they’re
actually watching.

00:04:00.140 --> 00:04:05.560
At the end of the day, viewers are there to
watch the video, not watch the sponsor.

00:04:05.560 --> 00:04:11.650
So, by nature, sponsors should only be incorporated
if they directly make the production possible.

00:04:11.650 --> 00:04:14.680
This is obviously the case with Mr. Beast.

00:04:14.680 --> 00:04:19.820
Each and every one of his videos costs millions
of dollars to produce, and even with a hundred

00:04:19.820 --> 00:04:24.199
million views per video, there’s no way
he’s breaking even with just YouTube adsense.

00:04:24.199 --> 00:04:29.570
In fact, he’s probably losing money even
with sponsorship money, but the sponsors make

00:04:29.570 --> 00:04:34.639
the loss much more manageable and affordable
when combined with Jimmy’s other ventures.

00:04:34.639 --> 00:04:39.830
In other words, you can go to any of Jimmy’s
vidoes, cut out the sponsored portion completely,

00:04:39.830 --> 00:04:44.600
and you’d still benefit from the extra production
quality that was made possible by the sponsorship.

00:04:44.600 --> 00:04:49.139
You don’t have to look that high up in the
YouTube hierarchy to find good sponsors either.

00:04:49.139 --> 00:04:52.810
Another channel with good sponsorship incorporations
is Magnates Media.

00:04:52.810 --> 00:04:56.479
If you’re not familiar with this channel,
you should absolutely check them out as they

00:04:56.479 --> 00:04:58.949
have extraordinary production quality.

00:04:58.949 --> 00:05:02.800
They basically produce full on documentaries
for free on YouTube.

00:05:02.800 --> 00:05:08.090
Videos like this can easily cost several thousand
dollars to produce if not more, so it’s

00:05:08.090 --> 00:05:10.539
no question why they need sponsors.

00:05:10.539 --> 00:05:15.460
Some other channels with solid sponsorship
integrations are Johnny Harris, Wendover Productions,

00:05:15.460 --> 00:05:16.960
Poly Matter, and Airrack.

00:05:16.960 --> 00:05:21.860
Now, let me disclaim, I haven’t watched
every video on all of these channels, so I

00:05:21.860 --> 00:05:24.610
can’t vouch for the reputability of their
sponsors.

00:05:24.610 --> 00:05:29.910
But, what I can say is that for their type
of content a sponsored segment is appropriate

00:05:29.910 --> 00:05:33.880
as it actually makes the rest of the video
better.

00:05:33.880 --> 00:05:41.720
For most sponsored videos though, this is
not the case which leads us to bad sponsorships.

00:05:41.720 --> 00:05:52.380
BAD SPONSORSHIPS:
A bad sponsorship is extremely easy to spot.

00:05:52.380 --> 00:05:56.270
In essence, it adds nothing to the video or
the production.

00:05:56.270 --> 00:06:01.440
And the niche that’s most notorious for
implementing such sponsors is the finance

00:06:01.440 --> 00:06:02.440
niche.

00:06:02.440 --> 00:06:07.740
As ya’ll are probably aware, the ad rates
on finance videos are through the roof.

00:06:07.740 --> 00:06:12.730
They’re usually able to earn $10 to $15
per thousand views from adsense.

00:06:12.730 --> 00:06:16.940
For larger channels, this means that they’re
able to earn as much as half a million per

00:06:16.940 --> 00:06:18.850
month just from adsense.

00:06:18.850 --> 00:06:23.680
On top of this, they’ve also got course
sales, affiliate links, YouTube memberships,

00:06:23.680 --> 00:06:25.930
patreon, and who knows what else.

00:06:25.930 --> 00:06:30.960
So, they clearly pull in an extraordinary
amount of revenue, but that’s just half

00:06:30.960 --> 00:06:32.560
the story.

00:06:32.560 --> 00:06:36.410
Not only do they have a ridiculous amount
of money coming in but they have virtually

00:06:36.410 --> 00:06:37.720
no production expenses.

00:06:37.720 --> 00:06:42.979
For the most part, finance videos consist
of just talking heads.

00:06:42.979 --> 00:06:47.050
Most of these channels can be run be a single
person and they are often run by a single

00:06:47.050 --> 00:06:48.050
person.

00:06:48.050 --> 00:06:50.340
At most, they may have an editor.

00:06:50.340 --> 00:06:55.669
So, no argument can be made that the production
needs sponsors or that sponsors make the rest

00:06:55.669 --> 00:06:58.729
of the video better in any notable way.

00:06:58.729 --> 00:07:03.400
If anything, the sponsor actually makes the
rest of the video worse because the YouTuber’s

00:07:03.400 --> 00:07:05.990
thoughts have to be censored to remain sponsor
friendly.

00:07:05.990 --> 00:07:10.790
In many cases, YouTubers aren’t even allowed
to mention or talk about other brands even

00:07:10.790 --> 00:07:13.569
in passing when a video is sponsored.

00:07:13.569 --> 00:07:18.580
Bigger brands are usually the worst offenders
and one of the best or I guess worst examples

00:07:18.580 --> 00:07:20.680
of this is actually Google.

00:07:20.680 --> 00:07:25.419
If you go to a Yes Theory video called “Running
for 24 hrs to see all of Paris”, you’ll

00:07:25.419 --> 00:07:30.520
see that a large portion of the video is filled
with a random blur effect on Ammar’s arm.

00:07:30.520 --> 00:07:35.540
The reason for this was confusing until you
reached the sponsored portion of the video.

00:07:35.540 --> 00:07:39.919
It turns out that the video was sponsored
by Google Pixel, and it’s likely that Ammar

00:07:39.919 --> 00:07:42.449
was using an Apple Watch and an iPhone.

00:07:42.449 --> 00:07:47.280
Google’s sponsorship team was probably not
super happy with this, so Yes Theory had to

00:07:47.280 --> 00:07:52.479
go back and add a blur effect throughout the
entire video and judging by the comments,

00:07:52.479 --> 00:07:55.470
this very much made the rest of the video
worse.

00:07:55.470 --> 00:08:00.060
Some people even went as far as claiming that
this alone made this video the worst video

00:08:00.060 --> 00:08:01.669
they’ve ever produced.

00:08:01.669 --> 00:08:07.759
Now, a constant blur effect is no doubt annoying,
but this issue becomes exponentially worse

00:08:07.759 --> 00:08:10.370
when we’re talking about finance content.

00:08:10.370 --> 00:08:15.289
Back in the day, finance content used to be
about index fund investing, house hacking,

00:08:15.289 --> 00:08:17.190
and maximizing credit card points.

00:08:17.190 --> 00:08:22.960
But, over time, more and more of the finance
community turned to popularizing cryptos,

00:08:22.960 --> 00:08:25.380
NFTs, and high risk options.

00:08:25.380 --> 00:08:29.930
During the worst of it, YouTubers were pitching
crypto brokerages left and right.

00:08:29.930 --> 00:08:33.649
Here’s some of the most notable ones that
you’ve probably heard of.

00:08:33.649 --> 00:08:38.279
Voyager Digital, Celsius, Block Fi, Genesis,
and FTX.

00:08:38.279 --> 00:08:44.120
But, these brokerages have more in common
that just being YouTube sponsorship favorites.

00:08:44.120 --> 00:08:47.180
All 5 of them are now bankrupt.

00:08:47.180 --> 00:08:51.830
I should mention that many of these YouTubers
lost money themselves because of these brokerages,

00:08:51.830 --> 00:09:00.800
but as Scott Shafer put it: YouTubers just
lost money, viewers had their lives ruined.

00:09:00.800 --> 00:09:09.589
UGLY SPONSORSHIPS:
If you thought pitching risky investments

00:09:09.589 --> 00:09:13.000
weren’t bad enough, it gets even worse.

00:09:13.000 --> 00:09:16.990
A select number of YouTubers have straight
up turned to scamming their audience.

00:09:16.990 --> 00:09:20.040
A great example of this was Roobet.

00:09:20.040 --> 00:09:25.200
If you’re not familiar with Roobet, it’s
basically an online casino, and being a casino,

00:09:25.200 --> 00:09:28.450
the odds are obviously stacked against players.

00:09:28.450 --> 00:09:32.970
Pitching something like this would already
be bad, but it’s worse than just that.

00:09:32.970 --> 00:09:39.150
You see, Roobet had an affiliate program that
essentially gave influencers 5 to 15% of all

00:09:39.150 --> 00:09:42.230
the money that their viewers lost by using
the platform.

00:09:42.230 --> 00:09:47.470
So, these influencers were literally incentivised
to have their viewers bet away all of their

00:09:47.470 --> 00:09:48.470
money.

00:09:48.470 --> 00:09:52.790
But atleast with Roobet, the viewers had the
potential of making money or atleast the exhilarating

00:09:52.790 --> 00:09:54.649
experience of playing those games.

00:09:54.649 --> 00:09:59.130
With something like Logan Paul’s CryptoZoo
NFTs, viewers were basically just handing

00:09:59.130 --> 00:10:02.180
over money to the influencer and the scammers.

00:10:02.180 --> 00:10:04.820
But, how did things get this bad?

00:10:04.820 --> 00:10:09.540
Well, of course the money is part of it, but
I don’t actually think that’s the main

00:10:09.540 --> 00:10:10.540
reason.

00:10:10.540 --> 00:10:15.110
The main reason is because of the classic
“I did it because he did it”.

00:10:15.110 --> 00:10:19.600
As audiences are exposed to sponsorships more
regularly, there tends to be less scrutiny

00:10:19.600 --> 00:10:21.300
and backlash.

00:10:21.300 --> 00:10:25.130
This allows a few brash YouTubers to push
the boundaries and pitch something that’s

00:10:25.130 --> 00:10:27.470
just a little bit more risky.

00:10:27.470 --> 00:10:31.190
Instead of pitching something like Vanguard
or Fidelity, you start pitching something

00:10:31.190 --> 00:10:33.070
like Robinhood or WeBull.

00:10:33.070 --> 00:10:37.380
If the audience doesn’t respond poorly to
these integrations, this gives bigger creators

00:10:37.380 --> 00:10:40.480
the confidence to start pitching these as
well.

00:10:40.480 --> 00:10:46.130
And before you know it, it’s normal to pitch
fintech companies, but why stop there.

00:10:46.130 --> 00:10:50.329
Once again, a few bold YouTubers, will push
the boundaries even more.

00:10:50.329 --> 00:10:55.220
If stock brokerages are ok, why not pitch
crypto brokerages.

00:10:55.220 --> 00:10:59.100
And if crypto brokerages are ok, why not pitch
NFTs.

00:10:59.100 --> 00:11:02.529
And if NFTs are ok, why not just pitch scams?

00:11:02.529 --> 00:11:06.660
And before you know it, you get to the situation
that we’re currently in.

00:11:06.660 --> 00:11:11.040
It really just comes down to how much the
audience is willing to tolerate and put up

00:11:11.040 --> 00:11:12.040
with.

00:11:12.040 --> 00:11:16.700
And some creators like Jake Tran are figuring
out the hard way that there is actually a

00:11:16.700 --> 00:11:18.630
limit to what viewers will tolerate.

00:11:18.630 --> 00:11:24.580
But, by the time they realize this, it’s
already to late.

00:11:24.580 --> 00:11:34.300
A BREAKING POINT:
The silver lining about all of this is that

00:11:34.300 --> 00:11:37.529
audiences are finally reaching a breaking
point.

00:11:37.529 --> 00:11:44.100
And with mainstream collapses like FTX, viewers
are more wise to sponsorships than ever before.

00:11:44.100 --> 00:11:49.070
Most viewers have come to accept that sponsors
are just a part of YouTube just like ads,

00:11:49.070 --> 00:11:51.730
and that you shouldn’t pay too much attention
to them.

00:11:51.730 --> 00:11:57.389
This sort of mentality is definitely for the
best, but sponsorships don’t have to be

00:11:57.389 --> 00:11:58.389
this way.

00:11:58.389 --> 00:12:02.970
As we discussed, there is a good way to do
sponsorships that actually improve the production

00:12:02.970 --> 00:12:04.820
and viewing experience.

00:12:04.820 --> 00:12:09.050
And with this breaking point being reached,
hopefully, more YouTubers will turn to value

00:12:09.050 --> 00:12:13.920
adding sponsorships moving forward and work
on making the best videos possible instead

00:12:13.920 --> 00:12:18.100
of just turning to the sponsorships that pay
the most and milking their audiences just

00:12:18.100 --> 00:12:19.640
to become even richer.

00:12:19.640 --> 00:12:22.110
But, that’s just what I think.

00:12:22.110 --> 00:12:24.290
What are your thoughts on YouTube sponsorships?

00:12:24.290 --> 00:12:25.639
Comment that down below.

00:12:25.639 --> 00:12:30.010
Also, drop a like if you would like to see
more videos exposing YouTube sponsors.

00:12:30.010 --> 00:12:34.360
And of course, consider checking out our discord
community to suggest future video ideas and

00:12:34.360 --> 00:12:40.490
consider subscribing to see more questions
logically answered.

